  "before_interfaces": {
    "lem-finite-weighted-upper-tail-pvalues-and-randomized-ties": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $(\\Omega,w)$ be a finite probability space, let $T:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$,\nand let $Z$ have law $w$. Define\n$$q(z):=\\sum_{\\omega\\in\\Omega}w(\\omega)1\\{T(\\omega)\\ge T(z)\\}.$$\nThen $\\Pr(q(Z)\\le u)\\le u$ for every $u\\in[0,1]$. If an auxiliary random\nvariable $U$ is independent of $Z$ and uniform on $[0,1]$, define\n$$q^+(Z,U):=\\sum_{\\omega\\in\\Omega}w(\\omega)1\\{T(\\omega)>T(Z)\\}+U\\sum_{\\omega\\in\\Omega}w(\\omega)1\\{T(\\omega)=T(Z)\\}.$$\nThen $\\Pr(q^+(Z,U)\\le u)=u$ for every $u\\in[0,1]$."
    ],
    "def-finite-randomized-treatment-assignment-model": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $N\\ge1$, and let $\\mathcal I=\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$ index the fixed\nexperimental units at the actual treatment-assignment level. Let\n$\\mathcal Z$ be a nonempty finite set of allowed assignments. If treatment\nis assigned to clusters, each cluster is one indexed unit; no separate\nprobability-sampling unit is present. The index $i$ identifies that unit,\nand no visit or repeated-measurement index is modeled here.\n\nA known assignment law is a function $p:\\mathcal Z\\to[0,1]$ satisfying\n$\\sum_{z\\in\\mathcal Z}p(z)=1$, so $(\\mathcal Z,p)$ is a finite probability\nspace ([[def-finite-probability-space-and-event]]); assignments of probability\nzero may remain in $\\mathcal Z$. A **potential-outcome schedule** is a function\n$\\theta:\\mathcal I\\times\\mathcal Z\\to\\mathbb R$, with\n$Y_i(z):=\\theta(i,z)$. The schedule and the units are fixed; the assignment\n$Z$ is the only randomness supplied by the design, and the observed response\nvector is $Y_\\theta(Z)=(Y_i(Z))_{i\\in\\mathcal I}$. The schedule is defined\nfor every allowed assignment, including zero-probability assignments, and\nmay depend on the full assignment vector, so no no-interference assumption is\nimplicit. The scientific statistical model contract\n[[def-scientific-statistical-model-contract]] requires the observation space,\nunits, quantity status, observation mechanism, support, conditioning\nconvention, and adequacy assumptions to be recorded; this model specifies\nthose fields as follows.\n\nTo express this as a statistical experiment, take the data space\n$\\mathcal X=\\mathcal Z\\times\\mathbb R^{\\mathcal I}$ with its full power-set\nsigma-algebra, which is a sigma-algebra by [[def-sigma-algebra]] and makes\n$\\mathcal X$ a measurable space ([[def-measurable-space]]), and parameter\nset $\\Theta=\\mathbb R^{\\mathcal I\\times\\mathcal Z}$. For each $\\theta\\in\\Theta$\nlet $P_\\theta$ be the law\n([[def-law-or-distribution-of-a-random-element]]) of the deterministic\nobservation map $O_\\theta(z)=(z,Y_\\theta(z))$ from $(\\mathcal Z,p)$. The map\nis measurable because its domain has the full power set, in the preimage\nsense of [[def-measurable-function-between-measurable-spaces]], and its law\nis a probability measure by\n[[lem-law-of-a-random-element-is-a-probability-measure]]. Thus\n$((\\mathcal X,2^{\\mathcal X}),\\Theta,(P_\\theta)_{\\theta\\in\\Theta})$ is an\nexperiment in the sense of [[def-statistical-experiment]]. The assignment\nlaw asserts no response-error model, Gaussian law, superpopulation\nrandomization, or sampling representativeness beyond the stated finite\nassignment mechanism. Its finite full-measure support set is\n$$S_\\theta:=\\{(z,Y_\\theta(z)):z\\in\\mathcal Z,\\ p(z)>0\\},$$\nsince $P_\\theta(S_\\theta)=\\sum_{z:p(z)>0}p(z)=1$. The record contains the\nassigned vector and every unit's response; missing or censored outcomes and\nrepeated visits require a separately declared observation mechanism. The\ninferential target is the assignment-invariance hypothesis for these $N$\nunits, not an estimand for a wider population.\n\nInference in this page holds $\\mathcal I$ and $\\theta$ fixed and averages\nover the declared law $p$; it does not condition on the realized assignment.\nThe status of the quantities is explicit: $\\theta$ and all\n$Y_i(z)$ are fixed, $Z$ is random, $(Z,Y_\\theta(Z))$ is observed, and\npotential responses under unassigned vectors are fixed but latent. The\nassignment set, weights, and implemented randomization protocol are\ndesign-controlled and should be checked against protocol records. Assignment\nlogs and response records are empirically assessable, but those checks do not\nprove that the declared law generated the data. Potential outcomes under\nunassigned vectors are fixed and latent. Their values are\n**substantively untestable** from the observed-data law; the\nassignment-invariance claim is a hypothesis tested by the theorem, not an\nempirical fact supplied by the design. These classifications do not make\nthe design or scientific model empirically true."
    ],
    "def-fisher-sharp-null-and-predeclared-test-orderings": [
      "## Definition\n\nIn the finite randomized treatment-assignment model\n[[def-finite-randomized-treatment-assignment-model]], a schedule satisfies\nthe **Fisher sharp null** when for each unit $i$ there is a value $y_i$ such that\n$Y_i(z)=y_i$ for every allowed assignment $z$. The full vector\n$y=(y_i)_{i\\in\\mathcal I}$ is then imputable under every assignment.\n\nA **predeclared ordering score** is a real-valued statistic\n$T:\\mathcal Z\\times\\mathbb R^{\\mathcal I}\\to\\mathbb R$\n([[def-statistic-and-estimator]]) fixed before the\nassignment and outcomes are inspected. Its upper-tail ordering includes ties:\nassignment $z'$ is at least as extreme as $z$ when $T(z',y)\\ge T(z,y)$.\nFor a signed score $S$ and fixed null center $s_0$, the corresponding\ntwo-sided ordering is explicitly $T(z,y)=|S(z,y)-s_0|$; for a no-effect\nsharp null the center is $s_0=0$. A lower-tail ordering is represented by\nusing $-S$ as the score. A statistic in this setting is a measurable map in\nthe sense of [[def-statistic-and-estimator]]; the full power-set data\nsigma-algebra makes every such real-valued map measurable."
    ],
    "def-finite-population-average-treatment-effect-and-weak-null": [
      "## Definition\n\nConsider the no-interference binary-treatment submodel of\n[[def-finite-randomized-treatment-assignment-model]] in which the allowed\nassignment set is $\\{0,1\\}^{\\mathcal I}$ and unit $i$'s response under\nassignment vector $z$ is $Y_i(z)=Y_i(z_i)$ for fixed real potential responses\n$Y_i(0)$ and $Y_i(1)$. For the fixed units, define the\n**finite-population average treatment effect** by\n$$\\tau_F:=\\frac1N\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal I}\\bigl(Y_i(1)-Y_i(0)\\bigr).$$\nThe **weak zero-average-effect null** is the hypothesis $\\tau_F=0$; it\nconstrains the average of the unit-level effects, not each effect separately.\nThe Fisher sharp null in this submodel, defined in\n[[def-fisher-sharp-null-and-predeclared-test-orderings]], requires\n$Y_i(1)=Y_i(0)$ for every unit. No probability distribution on the fixed\npotential responses is part of either definition."
    ],
    "thm-fisher-sharp-null-randomization-pvalues-are-valid": [
      "## Statement\n\nIn a finite randomized treatment-assignment model, fix any real-valued\npredeclared ordering score $T(z,y)$. For observed data $(z,y)$ define\n$$p_{\\rm rand}(z,y):=\\sum_{z'\\in\\mathcal Z}p(z')1\\{T(z',y)\\ge T(z,y)\\}.$$\nOn an extension with an auxiliary $U$ independent of the assignment and\nuniform on $[0,1]$, define\n$$p^+(z,y,U):=\\sum_{z'\\in\\mathcal Z}p(z')1\\{T(z',y)>T(z,y)\\}+U\\sum_{z'\\in\\mathcal Z}p(z')1\\{T(z',y)=T(z,y)\\}.$$\nFor every schedule satisfying the Fisher sharp null and every\n$u\\in[0,1]$,\n$$P_\\theta\\!\\left(p_{\\rm rand}(Z,Y_\\theta(Z))\\le u\\right)\\le u,$$\nwhereas the added randomization gives\n$$P_\\theta\\!\\left(p^+(Z,Y_\\theta(Z),U)\\le u\\right)=u.$$\nThus $p_{\\rm rand}$ is a valid finite-sample p-value for the sharp null,\nand randomized ties give an exactly uniform p-value when the additional\nindependent uniform randomizer is used. For a two-sided test, take\n$T(z,y)=|S(z,y)-s_0|$ with its center $s_0$ fixed in advance. The result\nuses the actual, possibly unequal assignment probabilities; random\nassignment supplies no Gaussian error law, and this theorem makes no\nvalidity claim under the weak zero-average-effect null from\n[[def-finite-population-average-treatment-effect-and-weak-null]] unless it\nis also Fisher-sharp."
    ],
    "cex-zero-average-treatment-effect-does-not-imply-fisher-sharp-null": [],
    "ex-unequal-weighted-two-sided-randomization-test-with-ties": []
  }
